# Cloudflare Workers static-assets header rules for apps/app.
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/headers/
#
# Copied verbatim from `public/` to `dist/` by Vite at build time;
# Cloudflare serves it as deploy-time routing metadata — nothing here
# ships as code to the browser.
#
# TIGHTER than the retired apps/defi's file on purpose: this app loads
# no Google Analytics and no external fonts, so script-src carries no
# third-party hosts and font-src needs no Google origins. The permissive
# `connect-src https: wss:` is inherent to a crypto frontend — RPC
# endpoints are env-configurable hosts, the indexer/agent Workers and
# the realtime push channel (wss://indexer.vaipakam.com/ws/chain/:id)
# are separate origins, and WalletConnect relays are third-party. Pin
# narrower only if the RPC set is ever frozen.
#
# `unsafe-inline`/`unsafe-eval` in script-src mirror defi (wallet SDK
# constraints); tightening to nonces is a follow-up hardening pass —
# do not loosen further without review.
#
# Safe-App embed support: `frame-ancestors` allows Safe's dapp-browser
# origins (this app registers the Safe connector). `X-Frame-Options` is
# deliberately omitted — it can't express an allowlist; modern browsers
# enforce `frame-ancestors`, which is the superset.
# `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin` lets the Safe parent
# frame (a different origin) read our resources.
#
# frame-src allows the WalletConnect verify iframe the wallet-connect
# handshake renders.
#
# `static.cloudflareinsights.com` is DELIBERATELY ABSENT (#1816), same
# ruling as apps/www's file records. If the zone has Web Analytics
# auto-injection on, Cloudflare inserts its RUM beacon into every HTML
# page response and this CSP refuses it, which surfaces as a console
# error on every page load. The fix is to turn the injection OFF at the
# zone — NOT to add the host here. The beacon is injected at the edge,
# downstream of the app, so the consent pipeline cannot gate it;
# allowing it would run an analytics collector before the visitor has
# consented to any, which the privacy spec forbids outright.
#
# Read that as a warning about a specific shortcut. `live-ux-sweep.mjs`
# now FAILS on the refusal (#1826), and adding the host here is the one
# edit that turns that failure green while making the underlying problem
# worse — the console error stops because the collector starts. If a red
# sweep sends you to this line, the change belongs in the Cloudflare
# dashboard, not in this file.
#
# RESOLVED 2026-08-22 (#1592) — the injection is now off, and where it
# lived is the part worth recording. There was no per-site entry to find:
# Web Analytics auto-injection is ZONE-WIDE, so a single site record on
# `vaipakam.com` covered every subdomain. All three hosts served the
# same beacon token, which is how that was confirmed. Turned off at that
# one record (Web Analytics → Manage site → RUM → Disable); verified 0
# on alpha02, vaipakam.com and defi, and the sweep went from a violation
# on every page to 54/54 routes with 0 console errors.
#
# So if it ever comes back, look for one zone-level record rather than a
# per-site switch — the search for a per-site entry is what stalled this
# the first time.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self' data:; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; connect-src 'self' https: wss:; frame-src 'self' https://verify.walletconnect.com https://verify.walletconnect.org; worker-src 'self' blob:; manifest-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'self' https://app.safe.global https://*.safe.global; upgrade-insecure-requests

# SPA entry point — served for every non-asset path via wrangler.jsonc
# `not_found_handling: single-page-application`. Force revalidation on
# every load so a deploy can never pin a client to stale JS that signs
# calldata against stale ABIs/addresses. The bundles themselves stay
# long-cached via /assets/* because Vite emits content-hashed names.

/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Immutable static assets (content-hashed filenames).

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Wallet-gated, per-user surfaces — never index. Header-level noindex
# so even a crawler that doesn't execute JS sees the policy (the SPA
# fallback serves index.html for these paths; SeoMeta's
# <meta name="robots"> only exists after hydration). Deliberately NOT
# robots.txt-Disallowed: a Disallow blocks crawling but not reference-
# only indexing of the URL — noindex requires the fetch to be seen.
# Every rule has a `/*` sibling: the router also renders trailing-
# slash and deeper variants, and _headers matching is exact
# (Codex #1309 r7). Keep in sync with SeoMeta.tsx + generate-seo.mjs.

/positions
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/positions/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/claims
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/claims/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/vault
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/vault/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/activity
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/activity/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/data-rights
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/data-rights/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/settings
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/settings/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/risk-access
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/risk-access/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
# /recover is additionally nofollow (Codex #1547 r1): the flow is
# deliberately unlisted, so its links must not lend it crawl equity
# either. Matches SeoMeta's follow:false for this route.
/recover
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
/recover/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
/faucet
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/faucet/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

# `/nft/:tokenId` — REAL pages, deliberately not indexed. A category of
# its own, and it is neither of the two below it: the children are not
# soft 404s, and the parent stays indexable.
#
# This block is round 56 P3 and it closes a gap rather than only
# correcting a comment. SeoMeta widened `/nft/*` from "noindex the
# malformed ids" to "noindex every token id" — token details are an
# unbounded url space of thin lookups and the sitemap has never listed
# them — but that decision is a META TAG, which only a crawler that
# executes JavaScript ever sees. The header is what the others read, and
# without this line they were free to index the whole space. A noindex
# that depends on JS execution is precisely the failure this file exists
# to prevent.
#
# `/nft` itself is absent on purpose: the verifier's entry point is
# indexable and is in the sitemap. `/nft/*` does not match it.
/nft/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

# Childless PUBLIC routes — the route itself is indexable, but any
# deeper path (`/borrow/anything`) is NotFound in the router: the SPA
# fallback still serves the index shell with a 200, so a non-executing
# crawler would otherwise index a soft 404 (Codex #1309 r8).
# `/positions/*` already carries noindex in the wallet-gated block
# above, and `/nft/*` in the block directly above this one — for a
# different reason, which is why it is not folded in here.

/borrow/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/lend/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/rent/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/offers/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/desk/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/vpfi/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/help/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
# #1959 — the two public transparency routes are exact and childless in
# App.tsx, so `/analytics/spam` is NotFound in the router. Cloudflare's
# SPA fallback still answers 200 with the index shell, and a crawler
# that does not execute `SeoMeta` would index that soft 404 — the same
# reason every route above is listed.
# REMOVED, not narrowed (round 37 P3). These two child splats existed to
# noindex soft 404s like `/analytics/foo`, but `*` also matches an empty
# remainder — so `/analytics/` picked up `noindex` while `SeoMeta` strips
# the trailing slash and correctly chooses `index: true`. A header cannot
# be undone after hydration, so the response won that disagreement and a
# valid form of a public URL was withheld from the index.
#
# Deleting them rather than rewriting the pattern, because the `/:locale/*`
# rule below already covers every path with a non-empty child segment —
# `/analytics/foo` matches it with first segment `analytics` — so these
# were redundant for the case they were written for. Narrowing with a
# `:placeholder` would have worked too; deletion needs no assumption
# about how the matcher treats an empty segment, which is the assumption
# that produced this bug.
#
# RESIDUAL — NOW MEASURED, AND IT RESOLVED AGAINST THE DELETION.
# This block used to say the empty-remainder question could not be
# settled from here. It can be, against the deployed app, and it was
# (2026-09-09, `app.vaipakam.com`):
#
#     /vpfi      200, no X-Robots-Tag
#     /vpfi/     200, x-robots-tag: noindex     <- `/vpfi/*` matched
#     /vpfi/foo  200, x-robots-tag: noindex
#
# So a splat DOES match an empty remainder, and `/:locale/*` therefore
# matches `/analytics/` too, with first segment `analytics` and an empty
# rest. Deleting the two child splats removed a redundant rule; it did
# NOT change what a crawler receives for the trailing-slash form, which
# is still `noindex`. The earlier claim that this was "strictly better"
# was wrong — the outcome is identical, and only the rule count moved.
#
# The same is true of EVERY single-segment public route: `/borrow/`,
# `/lend/`, `/rent/`, `/offers/`, `/desk/`, `/vpfi/`, `/help/`,
# `/analytics/`, `/protocol-console/` are all noindexed in their
# trailing-slash form by `/:locale/*`, whatever the child splats above
# do. Deleting five more rules would not fix it either, for the same
# reason.
#
# The actual fix is a REDIRECT, not a header — strip the trailing slash
# at the edge so only one form is ever served. That is a global routing
# change to the deployed Worker (a `_redirects` rule, or
# `html_handling: drop-trailing-slash`, which issues a 307 rather than
# the 301 canonicalization wants), and it cannot be verified before it
# ships. It is tracked as its own change rather than folded in here.
# Until then the consolidation signal is the canonical `SeoMeta` emits,
# which points at the slashless URL and is what a crawler follows.

# SPA aliases (App.tsx Navigate routes). The redirect only happens
# after JS runs, so a non-executing crawler fetching an alias gets
# the index shell — noindex ALL of them (Codex #1309 r1 + r2): the
# per-user-surface aliases (/dashboard, /manage, /loans*, /claim*,
# /vault-assets, /history) because their targets are never indexed,
# and the public-surface aliases (/earn, /offer-book, /trade,
# /terminal, /vpfi-vault, /nft-rental) because the alias URL is a
# duplicate of an indexable canonical target — the canonical route
# is the ONLY indexable address for that content.

/dashboard
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/dashboard/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/manage
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/manage/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/loans
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/loans/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/app/loans
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/app/loans/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/claim
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/claim/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/claim-center
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/claim-center/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/vault-assets
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/vault-assets/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/history
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/history/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/earn
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/earn/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/offer-book
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/offer-book/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/trade
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/trade/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/terminal
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/terminal/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/vpfi-vault
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/vpfi-vault/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/nft-rental
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/nft-rental/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
# The two aliases this PR added (round 38 P3). Both are single-segment,
# and `/:locale/*` below needs a first segment followed by more path —
# so neither is covered by it and both were served as an indexable 200
# shell to a crawler that never runs `Navigate`. `/nft-verifier` would
# then be indexed as a duplicate of `/nft`, and `/admin` as a duplicate
# of `/protocol-console` — or, where the console is hidden, as a public
# address for a page this deployment has chosen not to present.
# The `/*` partners cover a locale-free deep bookmark like
# `/admin/docs`, which the alias block above pairs for the same reason.
/nft-verifier
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/nft-verifier/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/admin
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
/admin/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

# ── Locale-prefixed aliases from the retired deployment ──────────────
#
# The old app mounted every route under `/:locale`, so `/es/positions/7`
# and `/de/analytics` are real bookmarks this app answers — with a React
# route that strips the prefix and re-enters the router. A crawler that
# does not execute the SPA never runs that redirect, nor SeoMeta, and
# receives the generic indexable 200 shell. The per-user rules above are
# EXACT paths, which a locale prefix walks straight past.
#
# ONE placeholder rule, not one pair per locale, and the reason is a
# hard limit: `_headers` accepts at most 100 rules. Enumerating 34
# locales took this file from 59 rules to 127, and the Cloudflare
# Workers build failed on both commits that did so while passing on
# every commit before them. It also never grows — adding a locale needs
# no edit here.
#
# `/:locale/*` matches a first segment followed by more path, so it
# cannot touch anything this app wants indexed: EVERY url in the
# generated sitemap is single-segment, which `src/lib/localeHeaders.test.ts`
# asserts precisely so this rule stays safe. What it does catch beyond
# the locale aliases is `/nft/:tokenId` — a per-token lookup with an
# unbounded url space that the sitemap already omits, so withholding it
# from the index is right on its own merits; the verifier's own entry
# point `/nft` stays indexable.
#
# RESIDUAL, stated rather than hidden: a BARE `/es` is not covered.
# `/:locale` as a pattern would match every single-segment path —
# `/borrow`, `/analytics`, the lot — so it cannot be used, and spelling
# out 34 bare paths would put this file back at 94 of its 100 rules,
# where the next locale or route silently breaks the build again. The
# cost is that `/es` may be indexed as a duplicate of `/`. That is an
# SEO nuisance, not an exposure: the bare prefix redirects to the public
# home page, and every per-user route is a deeper path the rule above
# already covers.
/:locale/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
